{
  "requirement": "A bundled skill MAY put material needed only in specialized situations in files it links to. Each such link MUST say when to read the file. Each bundled skill, recognized by its front matter, is a site:\n\n:::{sites} skills\n```yaml\nlanguage: markdown\npaths: [assets/skills]\nrule:\n  kind: minus_metadata\nmatch:\n  - \"---\\nname: example\\n---\\n\\n# Example\\n\"\nno_match:\n  - \"# Example\\n\"\n```\n:::",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Site query `skills`.** I ran `requirements.cli.skill_conventions.references/skills`. It returned exactly the five `assets/skills/*/SKILL.md` files, each with its `---` front matter: `habeas`, `habeas-requirements`, `habeas-evidence`, `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` and `habeas-refactor`.\n- A `---` search found front matter only in those five files. The `references/*.md` files have none, so the query correctly leaves them out. The requirement defines a skill by its front matter.\n- I found no bundled skill the query misses and nothing outside its scope.\n- The match snippet has front matter plus a body. The no_match snippet is a body with no front matter. Together they show what `minus_metadata` selects and what it excludes.\n- The requirement has no violation queries.\n\n**Obligations.** The requirement imposes one obligation: each link to a reference file must say when to read the file. Sites uphold it themselves through their site reviews. The test `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bundled_skills_say_when_to_read_each_reference_file]` adds a static check, which I assessed as follows.\n- **Each link has a pinned sentence.** The `links` table pins a \"when to read\" sentence for every `SKILL.md` link, and the test asserts the collapsed `SKILL.md` text contains each sentence.\n- **Reference-file pointers are covered.** The `reference_guidance` table does the same for pointers inside reference files: `review-prompt.md`, `reviews.md` and `distributed-requirements.md`. My search found a pointer in each of those three files and no others.\n- **Every pointer needs checked guidance.** For every skill in `SKILLS` (all five) and every file from `skill_files`, a regex finds each pointer. The test asserts that the pointer lies inside one of the pinned guidance sentences for that skill, or for that file. An unguided new link therefore fails the test.\n- **No link goes unchecked.** The test also asserts that every reference file a skill ships has at least one checked link.\n- **The regex covers every link form in use.** My search for non-HTTP markdown links (`](...)`) found only `references/*.md` and `../habeas/references/ci.md` forms. The regex matches both, along with backticked paths.\n\n**Result.** I found no gap in the site queries, their snippets or the test.",
  "references": "5b5a1f7cf171bc78f06adc8576f6e3e3dcb20c5f062af5c778e2b34a76dbfa84",
  "inputs": [
    {
      "requirements": "requirements.cli.skill_conventions.references",
      "matches": [
        "requirements.cli.skill_conventions.references"
      ],
      "text": [
        "::::{req} skill_conventions.references",
        "A bundled skill MAY put material needed only in specialized situations in files it links to. Each such link MUST say when to read the file. Each bundled skill, recognized by its front matter, is a site:",
        "",
        ":::{sites} skills",
        "```yaml",
        "language: markdown",
        "paths: [assets/skills]",
        "rule:",
        "  kind: minus_metadata",
        "match:",
        "  - \"---\\nname: example\\n---\\n\\n# Example\\n\"",
        "no_match:",
        "  - \"# Example\\n\"",
        "```",
        ":::",
        "::::"
      ]
    },
